Q: How to avoid using import-csv for huge sized files? A: <p>My earlier comment:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The question suggest that you didn't catch PowerShell pipeline <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.core/about/about_pipelines#one-at-a-time-processing" rel="nofollow noreferrer">One-at-a-time processing</a> concept. Meaning, if you have a huge <code>csv</code> file, I would recommend to use <code>Import-Csv</code> (the correct way) rather than avoiding it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>still stands, and is even a <strong>Strongly Encouraged Development Guideline</strong> to <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/scripting/developer/cmdlet/strongly-encouraged-development-guidelines#write-single-records-to-the-pipeline-sc03" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Write Single Records to the Pipeline (SC03)</a>. Besides as noted in the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/scripting/dev-cross-plat/performance/script-authoring-considerations" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PowerShell scripting performance considerations</a> document:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>❗Note</p>
<p>Many of the techniques described here aren't idiomatic PowerShell and may reduce the readability of a PowerShell script. Script authors are advised to use idiomatic PowerShell unless performance dictates otherwise.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I do although admit that there are exceptions as &quot;<em>it is not possible to use CSV and do the operations on top of it</em>&quot;⁽¹⁾, and issues as <a href="https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/issues/7603" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GitHub issue #7603</a> mentioned by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/45375/mklement0">mklement0</a> in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/60356120/45375">this answer</a><br />
The point is: once you start writing functions that read all data into the memory, you lose the opportunity to continue with one-at-the-time processing for other cmdlets in the pipeline.</p>
<p>Hereby a <a href="/questions/tagged/powershell" class="s-tag post-tag" title="show questions tagged &#39;powershell&#39;" aria-label="show questions tagged &#39;powershell&#39;" rel="tag" aria-labelledby="tag-powershell-tooltip-container" data-tag-menu-origin="Unknown">powershell</a> (as tagged) example of how to create a class that outputs an <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> as described in the helpful answer from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/15339544/santiago-squarzon">Santiago Squarzon</a>. The biggest issue in doing so, is that a <em>PowerShell</em> class doesn't have a <code>Yield</code> command to return the current item (see: <a href="https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/issues/5392" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GitHub issue #5392</a>), but that can be worked around by creating two stages (credits to the helpful <a href="https://gist.github.com/Jaykul/dfc355598e0f233c8c7f288295f7bb56" rel="nofollow noreferrer">gist</a> from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/8718/jaykul">Joel Bennett</a>).</p>
<p>The logic of the csv conversion depends on you actual data (aka <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comma-separated_values" rel="nofollow noreferrer">comma-separated_values</a> standard. Where the standard doesn't dictate the use of quotes or not.</p>
<ul>
<li>If your data fields aren't quoted (FORTRAN 77, approved in 1978), you might safe some time (as shown in the benchmark) to do simply something like: <code>$this.Line.Split(',').foreach{ $_.trim() }</code></li>
<li>Add the other hand, if this is about a custom parsing based on the <code>csv</code> standard, you might also consider creating a more sophisticated parser like the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.management.automation.psparser.tokenize" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PowerShell tokenizer</a> with allows for extensive <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.core/about/about_quoting_rules" rel="nofollow noreferrer">quoting rules</a> and different separators which comes of cause with some costs.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>using namespace System.Collections
using namespace System.Collections.Generic
using namespace System.Collections.Specialized
using namespace System.Management.Automation

Class EnumerateCsv : IEnumerator {
    [System.IO.TextReader]$Reader
    [string[]]$Header
    [string]$Line

    EnumerateCsv($Path) {
        $Path  = (Resolve-Path -LiteralPath $Path).Path
        $this.Reader = [System.IO.File]::OpenText($path)
    }

    [object]get_Current() {
        $Row = [OrderedDictionary]::new()
        $Data = $this.Line.Split(',').foreach{ $_.trim() }
        # $Data = [PSParser]::Tokenize($this.Line, [ref]$null).where{ $_.Type -ne 'Operator' }.Content
        for ($i = 0; $i -lt $this.Header.Count; $i++) {
            $Row[$this.Header[$i]] = if ($i -lt $Data.Count) { if ($Data -is [String]) { $Data } else { $Data[$i] } }
        }
        return $Row
    }

    [bool] MoveNext() {
        do {
            $ReadLine = $this.Reader.ReadLine()
            if ($null -eq $ReadLine) { return $false }
            if ($ReadLine.Trim() -eq '' ) { continue } # Skip empty lines
            if ($this.Header) {
                $this.Line = $ReadLine
                return $true
            }
            $this.Header = $ReadLine.Split(',').foreach{ $_.trim() }
            # $this.Header = [PSParser]::Tokenize($ReadLine, [ref]$null).where{ $_.Type -ne 'Operator' }.Content
        } while (-not $this.Line)
        return $null -ne $this.Line
    }

    [void] Reset() {
        $this.Line = ''
    }

    [void] Dispose() {}
}
</code></pre>
<p>You might convert the output to common PowerShell <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/scripting/learn/deep-dives/everything-about-pscustomobject" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>PSCustomObject</code></a> which are indeed quite heavy and slower:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>[ImportCsv]'.\file.csv' | Select-Object -First 10 | ForEach-Object { [PSCustomObject]$_ }
</code></pre>
<p>Or to your own class (based on mklement0's data example):</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>class MyClass {
    [string]$foo
    [string]$bar
    [string]$baz
}

[ImportCsv]'.\file.csv' | ForEach-Object { [MyClass]$_ }

foo   bar   baz
---   ---   ---
val11 val12 val13
val21 val22 val23
</code></pre>
<p>But than again, (based on your information) you might simply do (see <a href="https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/PowerShell-Docs/issues/12605" rel="nofollow noreferrer">document issue 12605</a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>Import-Csv '.\file.csv' | ForEach-Object { [MyClass]$_ } # ... | further processing
</code></pre>
<p>Creating a (lighter than <code>PSCustomObject[]</code>) <code>DataTable</code> (see: <a href="https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/issues/11941" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GitHub issue #11941</a>)</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>[ImportCsv]'.\Quoteless.csv' | ForEach-Object { $DataTable = [DataTable]::new() } {
    if (-not $DataTable.Columns.Count) {
        foreach ($Key in $_.get_Keys()) { $null = $DataTable.Columns.Add($Key,[string]) }
    }
    $null = $DataTable.Rows.Add([String[]]$_.get_Values())
}
</code></pre>
<p><sub>1. where I think there is more possible than suggested and issues often could be resolved with native PowerShell technics as <a href="https://devblogs.microsoft.com/powershell-community/mastering-the-steppable-pipeline/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mastering the steppable pipeline</a> for e.g. immediately diverting the output.</sub></p>
<hr />
<h4>Performance benchmark</h4>
<p>Creating a <code>DataTable</code> from a <code>150k</code> records <code>Csv</code> (with quoteless fields) in PowerShell 7.5.4</p>
<pre><code>1..5 | ForEach-Object {

$Stopwatch = [System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch]::StartNew()
Import-Csv .\Quoteless.csv | ForEach-Object { $DataTable = [DataTable]::new() } {
    if (-not $DataTable.Columns.Count) {
        foreach ($key in $_.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $null = $DataTable.Columns.Add($Key,[string]) }
    }
    $null = $DataTable.Rows.Add([String[]]$_.PSObject.Properties.Value)
}
$Stopwatch.Stop()
@{ 'Import-Csv' = $Stopwatch.Elapsed }

$Stopwatch = [System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch]::StartNew()
[ImportCsv]'.\Quoteless.csv' | ForEach-Object { $DataTable = [DataTable]::new() } {
    if (-not $DataTable.Columns.Count) {
        foreach ($key in $_.get_Keys()) { $null = $DataTable.Columns.Add($Key,[string]) }
    }
    $null = $DataTable.Rows.Add([String[]]$_.get_Values())
}
$Stopwatch.Stop()
@{ 'EnumerateCsv' = $Stopwatch.Elapsed }

}
</code></pre>
